# units.core — the baked SI base dimensions (LUNI.4.1.2).
#
# The seven SI base dimensions and their canonical anchors are PRIMITIVE: the
# coordinate axes of the dimensional algebra, always in scope and
# non-redefinable. They are the basis every other unit is expressed over — not
# standard-library content. The compiler pre-loads this module implicitly, so
# base literals (5_kg / 30_s / 100_m) resolve with no `import`; `units.si` and
# every other unit file build on these axes and MUST NOT redeclare a base axis
# ([LUNI.4.1.2]).
#
# Only these seven are baked. Derived SI (watt, Hz, …) and every non-SI system
# are earned by declaration in units.si and friends.
#
# Time is int64 nanoseconds; the other base dimensions are float64.

unit dimension time                storage int64    base nanosecond
unit dimension length              storage float64  base meter
unit dimension mass                storage float64  base kilogram
unit dimension temperature         storage float64  base kelvin
unit dimension current             storage float64  base ampere
unit dimension amount              storage float64  base mole
unit dimension luminous_intensity  storage float64  base candela

# The anchor unit of each base axis, with its canonical short alias, so base
# literals (5_kg / 100_m / 5_kelvin …) resolve with no import. These are the
# identity units (no conversion factor — they ARE the base). Scaled/prefixed
# forms on the other six axes (gram, kilometer, …) and the derived SI units
# live in units.si, which builds on these axes.

unit nanosecond:  time                label "ns"   alias ns
unit meter:       length              label "m"    alias m
unit kilogram:    mass                label "kg"   alias kg
unit kelvin:      temperature         label "K"    alias K
unit ampere:      current             label "A"    alias A
unit mole:        amount              label "mol"  alias mol
unit candela:     luminous_intensity  label "cd"   alias cd

# The `time` axis is baked IN FULL, not just at its anchor ([LUNI.4.1.2]).
# Duration literals occupy core grammar positions — cooldown / debounce
# ([LGRA.6.6]), after ([LTAC.2.1]), ttl, within, sliding, tumbling — so a file
# that imports nothing must still be able to write one. minute / hour / day are
# non-SI and are baked anyway, because the set an author reaches for is the set
# that has to be in scope.
#
# The canonical symbol is μs with U+03BC GREEK SMALL LETTER MU, the
# character Unicode prefers for the SI micro prefix. U+00B5 MICRO SIGN is the
# Latin-1 compatibility duplicate most keyboards emit, and `us` is the ASCII
# path; both are aliases so authors are not fighting their keyboard, but the
# label — what diagnostics and rendered values show — is the SI form. NFC does
# not fold the two mus (that is NFKC), so each is listed explicitly.

unit microsecond: time  = x * 1_000                     label "μs"   alias μs, µs, us
unit millisecond: time  = x * 1_000_000                 label "ms"   alias ms
unit second:      time  = x * 1_000_000_000             label "s"    alias s
unit minute:      time  = x * 60_000_000_000            label "min"  alias min
unit hour:        time  = x * 3_600_000_000_000         label "h"    alias h
unit day:         time  = x * 86_400_000_000_000        label "d"    alias d
